### Product cache-aside (Phase 6, unit 6.1)
### Start the lab stack: docker compose -f compose.yaml -f compose.labs.yaml up --build -d
### Passwords come from .env.

@gatewayUrl = http://localhost:8080

### 1. Login as the admin (root) to read and change products
# @name adminLogin
POST {{gatewayUrl}}/api/v1/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "username": "root",
  "password": "<ROOT_PASSWORD from .env>"
}

@adminToken = {{adminLogin.response.body.accessToken}}

### 2. First read: a miss, the database answers and Redis now holds the copy.
### Check: docker compose exec -T redis redis-cli TTL catalog:product:v1:1   (about 60)
GET {{gatewayUrl}}/api/v1/products/1
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken}}

### 3. Second read: a hit (catalog.cache{result=hit} goes up by one).
GET {{gatewayUrl}}/api/v1/products/1
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken}}

### 4. Update: after the commit the key is removed, the next read is fresh from the database.
PATCH {{gatewayUrl}}/api/v1/products/1
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "description": "edited from the cache lab"
}

### 5. Read again: a miss that returns the new description.
GET {{gatewayUrl}}/api/v1/products/1
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken}}

### 6. Redis failure: docker compose stop redis, then send request 5 again.
### Expect 200 from the database in under a second. Undo with: docker compose start redis
GET {{gatewayUrl}}/api/v1/products/1
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken}}
